diagnosis_text:
A thorough examination of all five routers and two hosts in this dc_clos_bgp topology finds no network fault. The reported symptom — pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) unable to reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) — could not be reproduced: 10/10 ICMP probes completed with 0% loss and sub-2 ms RTT, and traceroute confirms a clean four-hop path (pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_1 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1).

Every BGP session in the fabric is in the Established state with zero drops and zero notifications since lab start. All physical interfaces are UP/LOWER_UP with no errors or dropped packets. Both hosts have correct IP addresses and default gateways (pc_0_0 → 10.0.0.1, pc_0_1 → 10.0.1.1). IP forwarding is enabled on every router.

One design observation worth noting: no device has `maximum-paths` configured, so BGP installs only a single best path per prefix rather than ECMP across both spines. As a result, all leaf-to-leaf traffic travels exclusively through spine_router_0_1 (the "oldest-route" BGP tiebreaker wins over spine_router_0_0). This is an architectural limitation — if spine_router_0_1 were to fail, convergence would depend solely on BGP hold-timer expiry — but it is not causing the reported outage. There is no cable failure, no BGP session down, and no routing black-hole on any device.

The team's hypothesis of a physical cable failure between rack switches is incorrect. The network is operating normally. The user's report should be closed as a false alarm; no corrective action is required to restore connectivity.
=== END ===